From 581147842337ac355ab385fcc80e4ab6eff56558 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Gonzalez <dev@gonzalezerik.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 13:44:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 04/15] bsps/powerpc/ibookg3: Add BSD bus space support for PCI

The rtems-libbsd nexus bus turns PCI memory resources into bus space
through two BSP hooks.  RTEMS_BSP_PCI_MEM_REGION_BASE selects resource
activation for a host bridge whose PCI memory window maps one to one
into the CPU address map, as UniNorth does on both the QEMU mac99
machine and the real iBook.  RTEMS_BSP_READ_2/4 and
RTEMS_BSP_WRITE_2/4 replace the plain big endian accesses of the BSD
bus space helpers; the PCI address space reaches the CPU byte lanes
unchanged while the devices are little endian, so every access must
byte swap, done here with the libcpu io.h little endian accessors.

With these two hooks the FreeBSD gem (UniNorth GMAC) and ohci
(KeyLargo USB) drivers probe, attach and pass traffic through this
BSP on QEMU.
---
 bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md     | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/include/bsp.h | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 50 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
index f4fc9117aa6..f5b000a0c4c 100644
--- a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
+++ b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
@@ -22,6 +22,30 @@ What it uses:
   devices that the libblock and DOSFS layers mount
 * BATs: RAM cached, the mac-io/PCI space at `0x80000000` uncached and guarded
 
+Through rtems-libbsd (branch `bsp-ibookg3` of the workbench clone):
+
+* UniNorth GMAC Ethernet (FreeBSD `gem`, `miibus`, `ukphy`)
+* KeyLargo OHCI USB host controller (FreeBSD `ohci` via `ohci_pci`,
+  `usbus`, `ukbd`, `umass`)
+
+PCI MMIO is byte swapped: the BSP defines `RTEMS_BSP_READ_2/4` and
+`RTEMS_BSP_WRITE_2/4` as `in_le16/in_le32/out_le16/out_le32`, which the
+rtems-libbsd bus space uses for every PCI access, because the UniNorth
+bridge reaches the CPU byte lanes unchanged while the devices are little
+endian. `RTEMS_BSP_PCI_MEM_REGION_BASE` is zero (one-to-one mapping),
+which selects BSD bus resource activation for the PCI space.
+
+Notes for QEMU
+--------------
+
+* QEMU's sungem model only performs the software reset side effect on
+  its private `GREG_SWRST` register at offset `0x1010`; the FreeBSD
+  driver's reset write to `GEM_RESET` (`0x0010`) lands in the backing
+  store without resetting, so those bits never self-clear and the
+  driver prints `cannot reset receiver/transmitter` while the device
+  still works. On real hardware `GEM_RESET` self-clears and the
+  messages do not appear.
+
 Memory layout: the image is linked to run at 16 MiB (`0x01000000`) with load
 addresses starting at zero, which is what QEMU's `mac99` `-kernel` loader
 expects. The work area runs from the end of the image to the end of RAM as
@@ -59,4 +83,6 @@ Limitations
   Open Firmware (for example from disk) that places the image at 16 MiB.
 * No framebuffer console; the real iBook has no external serial port.
 * No PMU support (power off, real-time clock, battery).
-* No drivers for the other KeyLargo or PCI devices.
+* The KeyLargo ATA driver runs in PIO mode; DMA modes are untested.
+* The `gem` driver programs the GMAC through bus-space accesses and the
+  DMA descriptors in memory; it does not use Apple's DBDMA engine.
diff --git a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/include/bsp.h b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/include/bsp.h
index 796e5d24ef7..f2f2276e9bb 100644
--- a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/include/bsp.h
+++ b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/include/bsp.h
@@ -91,6 +91,29 @@ extern uint32_t ibookg3_ata_irq[IBOOKG3_ATA_COUNT];
 #define IBOOKG3_UNINORTH_CFG_ADDR 0x800000
 #define IBOOKG3_UNINORTH_CFG_DATA 0xc00000
 
+/*
+ * The UniNorth PCI host bridge maps memory space one to one into the
+ * CPU address map (the QEMU mac99 machine and the real iBook both do),
+ * so the bus region base is zero. This tells the RTEMS BSD nexus bus
+ * to provide resource activation for the PCI space.
+ */
+#define RTEMS_BSP_PCI_MEM_REGION_BASE 0
+
+/*
+ * PCI devices are little endian and the PCI address space reaches the
+ * CPU byte lanes unchanged, so a big endian PowerPC load or store must
+ * byte swap to present a little endian value to the device. The BSD
+ * bus space helpers use these macros for every bus space access.
+ */
+#define RTEMS_BSP_READ_2(_address) \
+  in_le16((const volatile uint16_t *) (_address))
+#define RTEMS_BSP_WRITE_2(_address, _value) \
+  out_le16((volatile uint16_t *) (_address), (_value))
+#define RTEMS_BSP_READ_4(_address) \
+  in_le32((const volatile uint32_t *) (_address))
+#define RTEMS_BSP_WRITE_4(_address, _value) \
+  out_le32((volatile uint32_t *) (_address), (_value))
+
 /* Used if Open Firmware does not provide the value */
 #define IBOOKG3_MACIO_BASE_DEFAULT 0x80000000
 #define IBOOKG3_MEMORY_SIZE_DEFAULT 0x08000000
-- 
2.55.0

